Z||000000||Johnson's85SE||01-29-2001||11:10 PM||||Hi,<BR> Just wondering if anyone had any ideas on getting the crud off of your windows when you take the tint off. What kind of cleaner should I use? Thanks,<BR>Chris<P>------------------<BR>If it isn't broke, it's not my car.||||reg||8
Z||000001||jch82||01-29-2001||11:25 PM||||ummm.........Goo Gone works nice! (and a razor)<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Before you criticize someone, walk a mile in his shoes. That way, if he  gets angry, he'll be a mile way - and barefoot....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||01-29-2001||11:35 PM||||This is my day, I work in a shop that does tint and autostarts.<P>The older the tint the tougher the glue gets.<P>On newer tint with defroster wires under it, spray with a soapy water solution and stick a black garbage bag against it and leave to soak for an hour, preferably in the sun.  Side windows about the same but doesn't need to soak as long because the adhesive can be scraped with a razor blade.<P>On really old tint the adhesive is very gummy and has become one with the glass.<P>White light duty 3M scuff pads and 000 steel wool liberaly doused with 3M adhesive remover used alternately as they clog will eventually get it off.  NOT fun but will get you there.||||reg||12
Z||000003||Cooter||01-29-2001||11:42 PM||||Once the tint film is pulled off, use a strong mixture of ammonia and water. It stinks, but is not very expensive or harmful to interior stuff like solvent based cleaners are. Also, there is a product specifically made for tint removal- I've seen it at detail shops. You could see what they would recommend.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Johnson's85SE||01-30-2001||12:17 AM||||Thanks for the info,<BR>Chris||||reg||1
